00:17:14.860 --> 00:17:21.340
So the better your audio is, the nicer it'll sound, the more everyone will be happy.

00:17:21.340 --> 00:17:23.500
Starting with a decent microphone is a good idea.

00:17:23.500 --> 00:17:28.300
It doesn't have to be a gigantic studio microphone, but something decent.

00:17:28.300 --> 00:17:31.900
I have a decent one that plugs into a USB port directly.

00:17:31.900 --> 00:17:33.740
And if you set the settings correctly,

00:17:33.740 --> 00:17:34.740
it does very nice sound.

00:17:34.740 --> 00:17:37.340
As I did not do one the first one, I had a setting wrong.

00:17:39.420 --> 00:17:41.740
And one I don't overload it, which I do all the time.

00:17:41.740 --> 00:17:45.500
So, you should take a look.

00:17:45.500 --> 00:17:47.820
So, Taj gave me some tips.

00:17:47.820 --> 00:17:50.620
I asked him for his top two tips.

00:17:50.620 --> 00:17:52.780
So I have three things to share with you.

00:17:54.340 --> 00:17:59.110
I tried, I tried.

00:17:59.110 --> 00:18:01.630
No.

00:18:01.630 --> 00:18:04.630
But thanks for the tips, because they're good.

00:18:04.630 --> 00:18:10.750
The three things are truncate silence, normalize,

00:18:10.750 --> 00:18:12.710
and compress.

00:18:12.710 --> 00:18:15.230
And these will give you a better range for your things.

00:18:15.230 --> 00:18:16.870
So we'll start with truncate silence.

00:18:16.870 --> 00:18:19.710
If you leave long gaps of silence,

00:18:19.710 --> 00:18:21.510
it's a good idea to cut that out.

00:18:21.510 --> 00:18:23.230
It'll cut the time down, and people

00:18:23.230 --> 00:18:26.470
will be waiting less for dead air.

00:18:26.470 --> 00:18:29.310
And all of my instructions are in Audacity,

00:18:29.310 --> 00:18:34.110
but they should be somewhat similar in other programs.

00:18:34.110 --> 00:18:35.830
Of course, Tenacity will be very similar.

00:18:35.830 --> 00:18:38.150
Other programs may have them in different places.

00:18:38.150 --> 00:18:43.110
But these are all pretty common.

00:18:43.110 --> 00:18:48.430
So you want to select all of your audio, Control-A,

00:18:48.430 --> 00:18:50.990
pulls in the whole audio after you've recorded it,

00:18:50.990 --> 00:18:52.830
with the default settings.

00:18:52.830 --> 00:18:55.470
So you do special and then truncate silence.

00:18:55.470 --> 00:18:57.790
There aren't any options, particularly in that one.

00:18:57.790 --> 00:18:59.110
And that will cut out the silence.

00:18:59.110 --> 00:19:01.790
If you do a podcast that's only two minutes,

00:19:01.790 --> 00:19:02.670
you don't really have to do that

00:19:02.670 --> 00:19:05.790
because there's no time for pauses in that anyway.

00:19:05.790 --> 00:19:08.230
So, but if you're gonna do something more drawn out

00:19:08.230 --> 00:19:09.830
and you need time to think,

00:19:09.830 --> 00:19:12.270
good idea to do truncate silence.

00:19:13.790 --> 00:19:16.670
Normalize, oh, I didn't bring the thing.

00:19:16.670 --> 00:19:18.830
So, normalize sets the volume.

00:19:18.830 --> 00:19:22.510
It brings the volume of different sections closer together.

00:19:23.350 --> 00:19:25.230
Especially important if there are more than one person

00:19:25.230 --> 00:19:29.330
talking or if the speaker's volume varies up and down.

00:19:29.330 --> 00:19:33.330
You want to select all the audio, go to effects,

00:19:33.330 --> 00:19:35.050
and then do loudness normalization.

00:19:35.050 --> 00:19:37.090
Now let me check one thing here.

00:19:37.090 --> 00:19:38.170
There is a setting for that.

00:19:38.170 --> 00:19:42.170
You want to normalize it to negative 6 dB.

00:19:42.170 --> 00:19:45.890
I checked with a professional for that one.

00:19:45.890 --> 00:19:49.530
But this is more important when your audio levels aren't

00:19:49.530 --> 00:19:55.340
completely consistent over the course of it.

00:19:55.340 --> 00:19:56.740
And then compression.

00:19:56.740 --> 00:20:00.100
So I hesitate.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:02.720
to do this because I hate what they do with pop music with this.

00:20:02.720 --> 00:20:05.040
It makes everything sound terrible and super loud

00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:08.960
and makes things unappealing to listen to, in my opinion.

00:20:08.960 --> 00:20:11.960
But for a podcast, if you were listening to someone

00:20:11.960 --> 00:20:15.160
in the car, compression could be the difference

00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:17.840
between being able to hear what the heck people are saying

00:20:17.840 --> 00:20:21.160
and all of a sudden they drift off and it gets too low

00:20:21.160 --> 00:20:23.040
and you can't hear anything and then you turn it up

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:24.440
and then it blasts out.

00:20:24.440 --> 00:20:29.440
It's not good, compression will help make that a little better.

00:20:29.440 --> 00:20:31.520
And there are a lot of settings in there,

00:20:31.520 --> 00:20:33.760
but the defaults seem to be okay with that.

00:20:33.760 --> 00:20:36.640
So again, select all your audio with control A,

00:20:36.640 --> 00:20:40.100
go to effects, volume and compression, and select compression.

00:20:40.100 --> 00:20:41.920
Again, there's a lot of options there,

00:20:41.920 --> 00:20:46.520
but I'm not aware of anything that this doesn't fix up

00:20:46.520 --> 00:20:50.850
on its own.

